meta-zybo-z7-10
================

This layer provides board support for the Digilent Zybo Z7-10 board.
It is intended for Yocto/PetaLinux based builds targeting the AMD Xilinx
Zynq-7000 SoC used on Zybo Z7-10.

The layer bundles the machine definition, hardware handoff, bootloader
customization, device-tree extensions, and kernel metadata needed to build
an image for the board from a single, self-contained layer.


Dependencies
============

This layer depends on the following Yocto layers being available in the build:

- `poky/meta`
  Core OpenEmbedded metadata required by all builds.

- `meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-core`
  Provides the Xilinx machine infrastructure and the base `linux-xlnx`
  kernel include used by this layer.

- `meta-xilinx-tools`
  Provides the `external-hdf` recipe used to import the local
  `system.xsa` hardware description into the build.

- `meta-petalinux`
  Required when using the XSCT based PetaLinux style flow enabled by this
  layer configuration.

At the layer metadata level, `conf/layer.conf` declares only `core` as a
formal dependency. In practice, the machine and kernel content in this layer
also rely on the Xilinx and PetaLinux layers listed above.


Layer Overview
==============

- `conf/machine/zybo-z7-10.conf`
  Defines the `zybo-z7-10` machine, selects `linux-xlnx-digilent` as the
  kernel provider, configures serial console defaults, sets U-Boot load
  addresses, and points the XSCT flow at the local `system.xsa`.

- `files/zybo-z7-10/system.xsa`
  Local hardware handoff archive consumed by `external-hdf` during the build.
  This keeps the hardware platform under version control with the layer.
  If you need to review or regenerate the XSA, refer to the Vivado project
  repository at
  `https://github.com/MainForm/LIG-Nex1_Team-Project_FPGA-Vivado`.

- `recipes-bsp/hdf/external-hdf_%.bbappend`
  Extends `external-hdf` so the local `system.xsa` can be located through
  the layer search path.

- `recipes-bsp/device-tree/device-tree.bbappend`
  Adds `system-conf.dtsi` and `system-user.dtsi` to the generated device-tree
  flow and extends kernel include paths used during device-tree generation.

- `recipes-bsp/u-boot/u-boot-xlnx_%.bbappend`
  Adds Zybo-specific U-Boot configuration fragments and installs
  `platform-top.h` into the U-Boot source tree during configuration.

- `recipes-kernel/linux/linux-xlnx-digilent_5.15_v2022.1.bb`
  Custom kernel recipe based on Digilent's `linux-digilent` tree. The recipe
  conditionally enables extra fragments such as `wifi.cfg`, `hdmi.cfg`, and
  `docker.cfg` when the corresponding `DISTRO_FEATURES` are enabled.


Machine
=======

The machine provided by this layer is:

- `zybo-z7-10`

This machine inherits the generic Zynq machine support from Xilinx metadata
and specializes it for the Digilent board by setting:

- the preferred kernel provider to `linux-xlnx-digilent`
- the serial console to `ttyPS0` at `115200`
- the XSCT/XSA-based hardware import flow
- board-specific device-tree includes through `system-user.dtsi`


Recipe Configuration
====================

Most of the behavior exposed by this layer is controlled through the machine
selection and `DISTRO_FEATURES`.

1. Add the layer to `bblayers.conf`:

   `bitbake-layers add-layer ../sources/meta-zybo-z7-10`

2. Select the machine in `local.conf`:

   `MACHINE ??= "zybo-z7-10"`

3. Enable board-related runtime features in `local.conf` as needed:

   `DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " wifi"`

   `DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " hdmi"`

   `DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " virtualization"`

4. Configure the SD card image layout with a WKS file when building `wic`
   images:

   The default Zybo Z7-10 WIC layout is selected from the machine
   configuration in `conf/machine/zybo-z7-10.conf`:

   `WKS_FILE ?= "zybo-z7-10_sd.wks"`

   `WKS_FILES ?= "zybo-z7-10_sd.wks"`

   The default layout is defined in `wic/zybo-z7-10_sd.wks`. The boot
   partition capacity is still controlled directly by its
   `--fixed-size` setting, but the root partition no longer needs a
   manually edited fixed-size value in the `.wks` file.

   The root partition uses BitBake's computed `ROOTFS_SIZE`, and that
   value is derived from the `local.conf` settings
   `IMAGE_ROOTFS_SIZE` and `IMAGE_ROOTFS_EXTRA_SPACE`. Because of this,
   changing those two variables automatically updates the final `.wic`
   root partition capacity. For example:

   - `IMAGE_ROOTFS_SIZE = "6291456"`
   - `IMAGE_ROOTFS_EXTRA_SPACE = "524288"`

   To change the root filesystem size, update only these values in
   `build_zybo-z7-10/conf/local.conf`:

   - `IMAGE_ROOTFS_SIZE`
   - `IMAGE_ROOTFS_EXTRA_SPACE`

   No manual `--fixed-size` update is required in
   `wic/zybo-z7-10_sd.wks`; the final `.wic` image picks up the new
   root partition size automatically during the build. If you need a
   different WIC layout for a local experiment, you can still override
   `WKS_FILE` or `WKS_FILES` from `local.conf`.

When these features are enabled, the kernel recipe pulls in the matching
configuration fragments from this layer:

- `wifi` enables `recipes-kernel/linux/linux-xlnx-digilent/wifi.cfg`
- `hdmi` enables `recipes-kernel/linux/linux-xlnx-digilent/hdmi.cfg`
- `virtualization` enables `recipes-kernel/linux/linux-xlnx/docker.cfg`

Device-tree customization can be done by editing:

- `recipes-bsp/device-tree/files/system-conf.dtsi`
- `recipes-bsp/device-tree/files/system-user.dtsi`

U-Boot customization can be done by editing:

- `recipes-bsp/u-boot/files/bsp.cfg`
- `recipes-bsp/u-boot/files/platform-top.h`
- `recipes-bsp/u-boot/files/user_2021-09-20-14-21-00.cfg`


Adding The Layer
================

Add the layer to `bblayers.conf`:

  bitbake-layers add-layer ../sources/meta-zybo-z7-10

Then select the machine in `local.conf`:

  MACHINE ??= "zybo-z7-10"


Notes
=====

- The current kernel recipe is based on Digilent's 5.15 kernel branch
  `digilent_rebase_v5.15_LTS_2022.1`.
- The hardware platform is supplied from `system.xsa` stored in this layer
  rather than fetched from an external URL.
- The Vivado project used as the reference source for the XSA is available at
  `https://github.com/MainForm/LIG-Nex1_Team-Project_FPGA-Vivado`.
- The device-tree flow uses the generated base device tree together with
  `system-conf.dtsi` and `system-user.dtsi` from this layer.
- This layer is compatible with the `scarthgap` Yocto release series.
